// ============================================================================
// burxt/bmx.bx — BMX 0.2, parsed into a typed tree.
//
//     use "bmx/burxt/bmx.bx";
//
// BMX is markdown with one unambiguous reading and a typed hole in it. The format is specified
// in its own repository, independently of this file and of this language: structure, error
// codes and a conformance suite as data. This is one implementation of it.
//
// ---- The one property that separates BMX from markdown -----------------------------------
//
// **It always fails loudly.** `*bold` with no closing star is an error here, not the characters
// `*bold`. An unterminated fence is an error, not a document that silently became one code
// block. `{{ x` is an error, not text. Markdown is designed so that nothing is ever a syntax
// error, which is the opposite of what a language whose compiler refuses things can build on.
//
// Every refusal carries a **code** — `BMX-E001` and its siblings — because a code is what a
// conformance suite can assert across implementations, while a message is this file's own
// words. The codes are the format's; the wording after them is ours.
//
// ---- Where this implementation stands ----------------------------------------------------
//
// The format defines two conformance levels. **Level 1 is rendering**: parse, then substitute
// slot values with escaping applied. **Level 2 is checking**: every slot expression verified
// against a declared interface before the document renders, so a slot naming a field that does
// not exist is a build error rather than a blank on a page.
//
// **This file carries both, and the split is in the file.** `bmx_to_html` is level 1: it
// refuses a slot with no binding and a link target with a dangerous scheme, but it looks values
// up by their expression TEXT and nothing there checks a type.
//
// **`bmx_emit_burxt` is level 2, and it is a code GENERATOR rather than a renderer — which is
// not a limitation but the design.** A `.bmx` document becomes a `pure function ... -> Html`
// whose slots are ordinary Burxt expressions, and then the COMPILER does the checking. Measured,
// all four: a slot naming a field that does not exist is a type error at the expression; a slot
// holding a `Decimal` rather than a `String` is a type error, so the conversion is written in
// the document where a reviewer sees it; money that would narrow without a rounding contract is
// refused *inside the view*; and `burxt review` diffs the generated signature between versions.
// `tests/runner.rs`'s `the_bmx_generator_hands_the_compiler_a_view_it_can_check` runs every one.
//
// **None of that is implemented here.** It is what the language already does to any function,
// which is `BOUNDARY.md` paying off: the format stays dumb enough for anyone to implement, and
// the checking lives where it can be enforced.
//
// One consequence of the same boundary, worth knowing before it is met: **a BMX document in a
// Burxt string literal has to escape every brace** — `"\{\{ user.name \}\}"` — because `{`
// opens a `{expr}` interpolation and a bare `}` closes one. It compiles and it is correct; it
// is merely unreadable, and `tests/pass/bmx_library.bx` is written that way on purpose to show
// what it costs.
//
// *(This paragraph first said a document "does not go in a string literal" at all. That was
// over-strong, and the fixture two directories away already disproved it. Corrected 2026-08-16
// — the weaker claim is the true one, and the habit of reaching for the stronger one is what
// put two wrong rows in `FAR-HORIZON-ROADMAP.md`.)*
//
// So BMX lives in `.bmx` files: read at runtime here, read at build time by the generator. Not
// because a literal is refused, but because a template inside one is a template no editor
// highlights, no formatter touches and no conformance suite can reach. The language decision
// behind this is `DESIGN.md`'s *"The delimiters do not move for an embedded format"* (2026-08-16)
// — the delimiters stay, and the format is the one with a choice left.
//
// ---- What it is built on -----------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `lib/html.bx` for output, so escaping has exactly one place to be right in this language and
// BMX does not get a second one. `lib/json.bx` for the AST, which is how the format's
// conformance suite compares implementations.
// ============================================================================

use "std/option.bx";
use "std/result.bx";
use "std/string.bx";
use "std/html.bx";
use "std/json.bx";
use "std/math.bx";

// ---- the tree ------------------------------------------------------------------

// A slot carries its expression AND where it started, because a host must be able to point at
// the source the author wrote rather than at whatever it generated. The format makes the
// offset mandatory for that reason.
public class BmxSlot { expression: String, offset: Int }

public class BmxWrap { children: [Bmx], offset: Int }

public class BmxLink { target: String, children: [Bmx], offset: Int }

// Inline content.
// An inline block — `::key[Ctrl+S]::`. NOT a slot: a slot's value is escaped and this is a call
// to something the host declared, so the two look different because they ARE different.
public class BmxInline { name: String, head: String, offset: Int, head_offset: Int }

public enum Bmx {
    Text(String),
    Emphasis(BmxWrap),
    Strong(BmxWrap),
    CodeSpan(String),
    Link(BmxLink),
    Slot(BmxSlot),
    InlineBlock(BmxInline),
}

public class BmxItem { children: [Bmx], offset: Int }

public class BmxHeading { level: Int, children: [Bmx], offset: Int }
public class BmxList { ordered: Bool, items: [BmxItem], offset: Int }
public class BmxCode { info: String, value: String, offset: Int }
public class BmxWords { children: [Bmx], offset: Int }

// Block content. `Paragraph` and `Quote` carry the same shape and are still two variants: they
// mean different things and render differently, and a `kind` field would put the distinction
// somewhere a `match` cannot see it.
// A block — the format's one extension point for structure. `head` is captured and NEVER parsed,
// exactly like a slot's expression: what `for line in order.lines` means is the host's business.
public class BmxBlock { name: String, head: String, one_line: Bool, offset: Int, head_offset: Int, children: [Block] }

public enum Block {
    Heading(BmxHeading),
    Paragraph(BmxWords),
    Quote(BmxWords),
    List(BmxList),
    Code(BmxCode),
    Fenced(BmxBlock),
}

// ---- errors --------------------------------------------------------------------

// `BMX-E001 at 6: unterminated slot`. The code leads so a conformance harness can compare on a
// prefix without parsing our prose, which is the half of an error the format owns.
pure function bmx_error(code: String, offset: Int, message: String) -> String {
    return code + " at " + to_string(offset) + ": " + message;
}

// ---- diagnostics, for editors and for hosts building on this ---------------------
//
// **`bmx_parse` answers a flat string, and that was enough for a command-line tool and is not
// enough for anything else.** `"BMX-E002 at 16: unterminated strong"` forces every consumer — a
// language server, a framework layering its own checks, a test harness — to re-parse BMX's own
// error text to find out where the problem is. A format that hands out prose and asks for it back
// parsed has moved work onto every host and kept none of it.
//
// So the same failure is available structured. The string form stays exactly as it was, because the
// conformance suite compares on the `BMX-Ennn` prefix and hosts already print it.
//
// **Line and column are here rather than in each host for one reason: getting them wrong is
// invisible.** A byte column looks right on every ASCII line and puts the caret in the wrong place
// on the first line with an accent in it — and the host that writes it will test with ASCII. So the
// column below counts CHARACTERS, and it is the same decision `char_at` and `substring` already
// make in this language.
//
// One-based, both, because that is what an editor shows and what `burxt` itself prints —
// `receipt.bmx:12:1`. **The LSP protocol wants zero-based**, so a server subtracts one; that is a
// deliberate choice to match what a human reads rather than what one protocol encodes.

public class BmxWhere { line: Int, column: Int }

public class BmxDiagnostic { code: String, message: String, offset: Int, line: Int, column: Int }

// Where a byte offset falls, in lines and CHARACTERS. Clamped rather than refusing: a diagnostic
// about a broken document is the last place to fail on its own arithmetic.
public pure function bmx_where(source: String, offset: Int) -> BmxWhere {
    let mutable line: Int = 1;
    let mutable last_break: Int = 0;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    let stop: Int = math_min(offset, len(source));
    while i < stop {
        if byte_at(source, i) == 10 {
            line = line + 1;
            last_break = i + 1;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    // Characters, not bytes: `substring` takes byte offsets and `char_count` answers characters,
    // which is exactly the pairing this needs.
    // `substring` takes a start and a LENGTH, not two offsets — the refusal says so if you
    // get it wrong, which is how this line was written the second time.
    let before: String = substring(source, last_break, stop - last_break);
    return BmxWhere { line: line, column: char_count(before) + 1 };
}

// The document's first structural problem, structured — or `None` if it parses.
//
// One rather than many, and that is §6 rather than laziness: BMX has no error recovery, so there
// IS no second error to report. A host that wants a list is a host that wants recovery, and
// `SPEC.md` §7 names the trigger for that.
public function bmx_check(source: String) -> Option<BmxDiagnostic> {
    match bmx_parse(source) {
        Ok(blocks) => { return Option.None; }
        Error(said) => { return Option.Some(bmx_parse_error(source, said)); }
    }
}

// Split `BMX-Ennn at N: message` back into its parts and place it.
//
// **This exists because `bmx_parse` throws the structure away, and it is the honest fix rather than
// the tidy one.** The tidy fix is to thread a diagnostic through every refusal in the parser, which
// touches every error site and would be a major for anyone matching on `Result<[Block], String>`.
// This recovers the three fields from a shape this file produces in exactly one place —
// `bmx_error` — so the two cannot drift without a test noticing.
// `pure` since Burxt 1.3.0, which marked `string_to_int` — the batch this file reported, when
// `json.bx` had zero pure functions out of eighteen, is in.
//
// **`bmx_check` above is still NOT pure, and the reason is not the one this comment predicted.**
// The prediction was that the standard library was the only obstacle. It was not: `bmx_parse_blocks`
// takes `mutable out: [Block]`, and `pure` with a `mutable` parameter is a contradiction the
// compiler is right to refuse — a contract clause may call a `pure` function, so allowing it would
// let a precondition rewrite the data it checks. So `bmx_parse` and everything through it stays
// impure until that accumulator becomes a return value, which is MY design to change, not Burxt's.
pure function bmx_parse_error(source: String, said: String) -> BmxDiagnostic {
    let at: Int = string_find(said, " at ");
    let colon: Int = string_find(said, ": ");
    if at < 0 || colon < at {
        return BmxDiagnostic { code: "BMX-E000", message: said, offset: 0, line: 1, column: 1 };
    }
    let code: String = substring(said, 0, at);
    let offset: Int = string_to_int(substring(said, at + 4, colon - (at + 4)), 0);
    let message: String = substring(said, colon + 2, len(said) - (colon + 2));
    let where: BmxWhere = bmx_where(source, offset);
    return BmxDiagnostic {
        code: code,
        message: message,
        offset: offset,
        line: where.line,
        column: where.column,
    };
}

// ---- scanning helpers ----------------------------------------------------------

pure function bmx_is_space(b: Int) -> Bool {
    return b == 32 || b == 9;
}

// A line's content with trailing spaces removed. The format strips them because the two-space
// line break is an invisible character that changes output, which is unreviewable by
// construction.
// Removes up to `n` leading spaces — the opening fence's indentation and no more. A content line
// indented LESS than its fence keeps what it has rather than going negative.
pure function bmx_strip_fence_indent(raw: String, n: Int) -> String {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < n && i < len(raw) && byte_at(raw, i) == 32 {
        i += 1;
    }
    return substring(raw, i, len(raw) - i);
}

public pure function bmx_strip_end(text: String) -> String {
    let mutable n: Int = len(text);
    while n > 0 && byte_at(text, n - 1) == 32 {
        n -= 1;
    }
    return substring(text, 0, n);
}

pure function bmx_is_blank(text: String) -> Bool {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(text) {
        if byte_at(text, i) != 32 { return false; }
        i += 1;
    }
    return true;
}

pure function bmx_starts_with(text: String, prefix: String) -> Bool {
    return string_starts_with(text, prefix);
}

// The digits at the start of a line, or -1 if there are none. `12. ` is an ordered marker.
pure function bmx_ordered_marker(text: String) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(text) && byte_at(text, i) >= 48 && byte_at(text, i) <= 57 {
        i += 1;
    }
    if i == 0 { return 0 - 1; }
    if i + 1 >= len(text) { return 0 - 1; }
    if byte_at(text, i) != 46 { return 0 - 1; }
    if byte_at(text, i + 1) != 32 { return 0 - 1; }
    return i + 2;
}

// **A block opens with `:name:` and closes with `:!name:`, and nesting is by NAME.** 0.6 nested by
// fence length — a longer `::::` contained a shorter `:::` — and that rule is gone along with the
// counting it asked of a reader. Andre's spelling; the argument for it is one line of a real
// component, where `:!button:` says what it closes and `:::` said only *something ends here*.

// The index of the colon that ends a `:xxx:` marker, or -1 when the line is not reaching for one.
// Used for the opener AND for the refusal when what is between the colons is not a name.
pure function bmx_marker_end(text: String) -> Int {
    if len(text) < 3 { return 0 - 1; }
    if byte_at(text, 0) != 58 { return 0 - 1; }
    // `::` is an inline block or 0.6's fence, `:!` is a closer: neither is an opener.
    if byte_at(text, 1) == 58 || byte_at(text, 1) == 33 { return 0 - 1; }
    let mutable i: Int = 1;
    while i < len(text) && byte_at(text, i) != 58 {
        i += 1;
    }
    if i >= len(text) { return 0 - 1; }
    return i;
}

// The name in `:!name:`, or "" when the line is not a closer. A closer stands alone on its line.
pure function bmx_closer_name(text: String) -> String {
    let trimmed: String = bmx_strip_end(text);
    if len(trimmed) < 4 { return ""; }
    if byte_at(trimmed, 0) != 58 || byte_at(trimmed, 1) != 33 { return ""; }
    if byte_at(trimmed, len(trimmed) - 1) != 58 { return ""; }
    let name: String = substring(trimmed, 2, len(trimmed) - 3);
    if !html_is_name(name) { return ""; }
    return name;
}

// 0.6's fence, recognised ONLY so it can be refused by name. A 0.6 document fed to 0.7 would
// otherwise render its fences as paragraph text, and not doing that is the format's whole claim.
pure function bmx_is_old_fence(text: String) -> Bool {
    return len(text) >= 3 && byte_at(text, 0) == 58 && byte_at(text, 1) == 58 && byte_at(text, 2) == 58;
}

// ---- inline parsing ------------------------------------------------------------

// `base` is where `text` starts in the whole document, so a slot's offset is a real position in
// the file the author opened rather than an index into a fragment.
pure function bmx_parse_inline(text: String, base: Int) -> Result<[Bmx], String> {
    let mutable out: [Bmx] = [];
    let mutable buffer: String = "";
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    let n: Int = len(text);
    while i < n {
        let b: Int = byte_at(text, i);

        // The escape. One rule, and a backslash before anything else is an error rather than
        // sometimes-a-backslash — that ambiguity is where markdown dialects diverge.
        if b == 92 {
            if i + 1 >= n {
                return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E021", base + i, "a backslash at the end of a line escapes nothing"));
            }
            let next: Int = byte_at(text, i + 1);
            let escapable: Bool = next == 96 || next == 42 || next == 91 || next == 123 || next == 92;
            if !escapable {
                return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E021", base + i, "only ` * [ \{ and \\ may be escaped"));
            }
            buffer = buffer + byte_as_string(next);
            i += 2;
        } else {
            if b == 123 && i + 1 < n && byte_at(text, i + 1) == 123 {
                // A slot. Its `}}` must be on this line — the format says so, and a slot that
                // could span lines is a slot whose unterminated case looks like a paragraph.
                let close: Int = bmx_slot_end(text, i + 2);
                // A slot must close on its own line. Searching the joined paragraph text
                // without this would find a `\}\}` two lines down and silently swallow
                // everything between — the exact shape of failure BMX exists to not have.
                let newline: Int = string_find_from(text, "\n", i + 2);
                let runs_on: Bool = newline >= 0 && (close < 0 || newline < close);
                if close < 0 || runs_on {
                    return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E001", base + i, "unterminated slot: no \}\} on this line, outside a quoted value"));
                }
                let raw: String = substring(text, i + 2, close - i - 2);
                let expression: String = string_trim(raw);
                // The offset points at the first byte of the EXPRESSION, not at the `\{\{`, so
                // a host underlines the text it is complaining about. `BOUNDARY.md` requires
                // an offset into the author's source for exactly this reason.
                let mutable pad: Int = 0;
                while pad < len(raw) && bmx_is_space(byte_at(raw, pad)) {
                    pad += 1;
                }
                if len(expression) == 0 {
                    return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E022", base + i, "a slot's expression is empty"));
                }
                if len(buffer) > 0 {
                    let flushed: Int = push(out, Bmx.Text(buffer));
                    buffer = "";
                }
                let added: Int = push(out, Bmx.Slot(BmxSlot { expression: expression, offset: base + i + 2 + pad }));
                i = close + 2;
            } else {
                if b == 96 {
                    let close: Int = string_find_from(text, "`", i + 1);
                    if close < 0 {
                        return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E020", base + i, "unterminated code span"));
                    }
                    if len(buffer) > 0 {
                        let flushed: Int = push(out, Bmx.Text(buffer));
                        buffer = "";
                    }
                    let added: Int = push(out, Bmx.CodeSpan(substring(text, i + 1, close - i - 1)));
                    i = close + 1;
                } else {
                    if b == 42 {
                        let strong: Bool = i + 1 < n && byte_at(text, i + 1) == 42;
                        let marker: String = "*";
                        let width: Int = 1;
                        if strong {
                            let close: Int = string_find_from(text, "**", i + 2);
                            if close < 0 {
                                return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E002", base + i, "unterminated strong"));
                            }
                            if len(buffer) > 0 {
                                let flushed: Int = push(out, Bmx.Text(buffer));
                                buffer = "";
                            }
                            match bmx_parse_inline(substring(text, i + 2, close - i - 2), base + i + 2) {
                                Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                                Ok(kids) => {
                                    let added: Int = push(out, Bmx.Strong(BmxWrap { children: kids, offset: base + i }));
                                }
                            }
                            i = close + 2;
                        } else {
                            let close: Int = string_find_from(text, "*", i + 1);
                            if close < 0 {
                                return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E002", base + i, "unterminated emphasis"));
                            }
                            if len(buffer) > 0 {
                                let flushed: Int = push(out, Bmx.Text(buffer));
                                buffer = "";
                            }
                            match bmx_parse_inline(substring(text, i + 1, close - i - 1), base + i + 1) {
                                Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                                Ok(kids) => {
                                    let added: Int = push(out, Bmx.Emphasis(BmxWrap { children: kids, offset: base + i }));
                                }
                            }
                            i = close + 1;
                        }
                    } else {
                        if b == 58 && i + 1 < n && byte_at(text, i + 1) == 58 {
                            // `::name[head]::` — a call to something the host declared. It is not
                            // a slot: a slot's value is escaped, this one's is the host's to
                            // decide, and a reader must see the difference without looking it up.
                            let open: Int = string_find_from(text, "[", i + 2);
                            if open < 0 {
                                return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E034", base + i, "an inline block is ::name[head]::"));
                            }
                            let name: String = substring(text, i + 2, open - i - 2);
                            if !html_is_name(name) {
                                return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E034", base + i, "an inline block is ::name[head]::"));
                            }
                            let shut: Int = string_find_from(text, "]::", open + 1);
                            if shut < 0 {
                                return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E034", base + i, "unterminated inline block: no ]:: on this line"));
                            }
                            if len(buffer) > 0 {
                                let flushed: Int = push(out, Bmx.Text(buffer));
                                buffer = "";
                            }
                            let added: Int = push(out, Bmx.InlineBlock(BmxInline {
                                name: name,
                                head: substring(text, open + 1, shut - open - 1),
                                offset: base + i,
                                head_offset: base + open + 1,
                            }));
                            i = shut + 3;
                        } else {
                        if b == 91 {
                            let shut: Int = string_find_from(text, "]", i + 1);
                            if shut < 0 {
                                return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E004", base + i, "unterminated link text"));
                            }
                            if shut + 1 >= n || byte_at(text, shut + 1) != 40 {
                                return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E004", base + i, "a link's text must be followed by (target)"));
                            }
                            let end: Int = bmx_target_end(text, shut + 2);
                            if end < 0 {
                                return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E004", base + i,
                                    "unterminated link target: its parentheses do not balance, so there is no `)` that ends it"));
                            }
                            // **A slot in a target was emitted as LITERAL TEXT**, so `[Home]({{ url }})`
                            // produced `href="{{ url }}"` — a broken link, rendered, with nothing said.
                            // Refused rather than supported: substituting a bound value into a target
                            // means the scheme check must run AFTER substitution, which is a design
                            // decision. §7 carries the same open question for images.
                            let target_text: String = substring(text, shut + 2, end - (shut + 2));
                            if string_find(target_text, "\{\{") >= 0 {
                                return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E005", base + i,
                                    "a link target is opaque, so a slot in one is not substituted — it would render as the characters `\{\{ … \}\}`. Use a block your host declares"));
                            }
                            if len(buffer) > 0 {
                                let flushed: Int = push(out, Bmx.Text(buffer));
                                buffer = "";
                            }
                            match bmx_parse_inline(substring(text, i + 1, shut - i - 1), base + i + 1) {
                                Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                                Ok(kids) => {
                                    let target: String = substring(text, shut + 2, end - shut - 2);
                                    let added: Int = push(out, Bmx.Link(BmxLink { target: target, children: kids, offset: base + i }));
                                }
                            }
                            i = end + 1;
                        } else {
                            buffer = buffer + byte_as_string(b);
                            i += 1;
                        }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    if len(buffer) > 0 {
        let flushed: Int = push(out, Bmx.Text(buffer));
    }
    return Result.Ok(out);
}

// Adjacent `Text` nodes are always merged. The format requires it — two implementations that
// disagree about whether `a` `b` is one node or two disagree about the document, and the
// conformance suite would rightly fail one of them.
//
// This exists because inline content is parsed **one line at a time** rather than over a joined
// buffer. That is what keeps a slot's offset pointing at the author's source: joining first and
// parsing after put the offset off by exactly the trailing spaces stripped from every earlier
// line, measured at 11 where the byte was at 14. It is also what the spec already implied —
// every inline construct must close on its own line, so there was never a reason to parse
// across one.
pure function bmx_merge_text(nodes: [Bmx]) -> [Bmx] {
    let mutable out: [Bmx] = [];
    let mutable pending: String = "";
    let mutable have: Bool = false;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(nodes) {
        match nodes[i] {
            Text(value) => {
                pending = pending + value;
                have = true;
            }
            Emphasis(wrap) => {
                if have { let f: Int = push(out, Bmx.Text(pending)); pending = ""; have = false; }
                let added: Int = push(out, nodes[i]);
            }
            Strong(wrap) => {
                if have { let f: Int = push(out, Bmx.Text(pending)); pending = ""; have = false; }
                let added: Int = push(out, nodes[i]);
            }
            CodeSpan(value) => {
                if have { let f: Int = push(out, Bmx.Text(pending)); pending = ""; have = false; }
                let added: Int = push(out, nodes[i]);
            }
            Link(link) => {
                if have { let f: Int = push(out, Bmx.Text(pending)); pending = ""; have = false; }
                let added: Int = push(out, nodes[i]);
            }
            Slot(slot) => {
                if have { let f: Int = push(out, Bmx.Text(pending)); pending = ""; have = false; }
                let added: Int = push(out, nodes[i]);
            }
            InlineBlock(call) => {
                if have { let f: Int = push(out, Bmx.Text(pending)); pending = ""; have = false; }
                let added: Int = push(out, nodes[i]);
            }
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    if have { let f: Int = push(out, Bmx.Text(pending)); }
    return out;
}

// ---- lines ---------------------------------------------------------------------

class BmxLine { text: String, raw: String, indent: Int, offset: Int }

// Split into lines, keeping each line's byte offset. `\r\n` ends a line and a lone `\r` does
// not — a stray carriage return in the middle of a line is far more likely to be data than
// intent, and the format says so rather than leaving it to each parser.
pure function bmx_lines(source: String) -> [BmxLine] {
    let mutable out: [BmxLine] = [];
    let n: Int = len(source);
    let mutable start: Int = 0;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < n {
        if byte_at(source, i) == 10 {
            let mutable stop: Int = i;
            if stop > start && byte_at(source, stop - 1) == 13 {
                stop -= 1;
            }
            let added: Int = push(out, bmx_row(substring(source, start, stop - start), start));
            i += 1;
            start = i;
        } else {
            i += 1;
        }
    }
    if start < n {
        let added: Int = push(out, bmx_row(substring(source, start, n - start), start));
    }
    return out;
}

// **Leading spaces come off HERE and nowhere else**, which is the whole implementation of SPEC §1's
// insignificance rule. Doing it once at the row rather than at each of the twenty places that read a
// line means every offset downstream is already a real source position — the alternative is
// `offset + indent` at each of them, which is twenty chances to forget one and hand the language
// server a column pointing at whitespace.
//
// `raw` is kept because a code block's content is the one thing that must not be dedented line by
// line: it loses its FENCE's indentation and keeps the rest (§2.4).
pure function bmx_row(raw: String, offset: Int) -> BmxLine {
    let mutable n: Int = 0;
    while n < len(raw) && byte_at(raw, n) == 32 {
        n += 1;
    }
    return BmxLine {
        text: substring(raw, n, len(raw) - n),
        raw: raw,
        indent: n,
        offset: offset + n,
    };
}

// The nesting refusal, in one place with three callers — three loops consume lines and each consumes
// its own, so the top of the block loop never sees a continuation line. The FIXTURES are what hold
// this rather than the structure: `- one` / `  - two` silently became a flat list until
// `014-nested-list` failed, and the quote shape had no fixture at all until this pass added one.
pure function bmx_no_nesting(offset: Int, what: String, fix: String) -> String {
    return bmx_error("BMX-E012", offset,
        "a " + what + " may not nest; this line is indented. " + fix + " — see §4a.2");
}

// **Three delimiters in this format and all three needed the same thing said about them.** A head ended
// at the first `]`, a slot at the first `}}`, a link target at the first `)` — and each delimiter's own
// characters appear legitimately inside. Every result was silent, which is what makes it one defect:
//
//     {{ pick("}}", n) }}       expression became `pick("`
//     [Foo](/wiki/Foo_(bar))    target became `/wiki/Foo_(bar`
//
// **A delimiter rule has to know what protects a delimiter.** What protects what is semantic rather than
// arbitrary: a head and a slot hold host expressions, where a `"` run is a string; a link target is a
// URL, where parentheses nest. When the protection never closes, all three refuse.

// The first `}}` outside a quoted run, or -1.
pure function bmx_slot_end(text: String, from: Int) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = from;
    let mutable quoted: Bool = false;
    while i < len(text) {
        let here: Int = byte_at(text, i);
        if quoted {
            if here == 34 {
                quoted = false;
            }
        } else {
            if here == 34 {
                quoted = true;
            } else {
                if here == 125 && i + 1 < len(text) && byte_at(text, i + 1) == 125 {
                    return i;
                }
            }
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

// The `)` that balances the target's opening `(`, or -1. A URL's parentheses nest, which is
// CommonMark's rule rather than an invention; a quoted run does not arise, since a target is not an
// expression.
pure function bmx_target_end(text: String, from: Int) -> Int {
    let mutable depth: Int = 1;
    let mutable i: Int = from;
    while i < len(text) {
        let here: Int = byte_at(text, i);
        if here == 40 {
            depth += 1;
        }
        if here == 41 {
            depth -= 1;
            if depth == 0 {
                return i;
            }
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

// Where a delimited head ends: the first `]` that is not inside a quoted value or a slot.
//
// **The first version took the first `]` at all and called that an escape hatch.** It was a SILENT
// truncation — `-> [title="a]b"] hi` became head `title="a` with body `b"] hi`, and
// `-> [class={{ tags[0] }}] hi` became head `class={{ tags[0` — which is the defect class this format
// exists to remove, shipped inside a feature an hour old. An escape hatch nobody is told about is a trap.
//
// The scanner knows exactly two things about head content and nothing else: a `"` run protects what is
// inside it, and a `{{ … }}` run does too. That is not parsing the head's structure; it is what any
// bracket matcher must know to find its own partner. An unterminated `"` or `{{` reaches end of line
// with no unprotected `]`, which answers `BMX-E037` — loud, and pointing at the line that is wrong.
pure function bmx_head_end(text: String, from: Int) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = from;
    let mutable quoted: Bool = false;
    let mutable slot: Bool = false;
    while i < len(text) {
        let here: Int = byte_at(text, i);
        let mutable next: Int = 0;
        if i + 1 < len(text) {
            next = byte_at(text, i + 1);
        }
        if slot {
            if here == 125 && next == 125 {
                slot = false;
                i += 1;
            }
        } else {
            if quoted {
                if here == 34 {
                    quoted = false;
                }
            } else {
                if here == 123 && next == 123 {
                    slot = true;
                    i += 1;
                } else {
                    if here == 34 {
                        quoted = true;
                    } else {
                        if here == 93 {
                            return i;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

// ---- block parsing -------------------------------------------------------------

// A document, or the first error in it. A conforming parser stops at the first error: recovery
// is a real want, but recovery that differs between implementations is worse than none.
public function bmx_parse(source: String) -> Result<[Block], String> {
    let lines: [BmxLine] = bmx_lines(source);
    let mutable out: [Block] = [];
    match bmx_parse_blocks(lines, 0, "", 0 - 1, out) {
        Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
        Ok(stop) => {
            // The reader only stops early on a closer, and at the top level nothing is open.
            if stop < len(lines) {
                return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E032", lines[stop].offset,
                    "a closer with no open block"));
            }
            return Result.Ok(out);
        }
    }
}

// `open` is the enclosing block's NAME, or "" at the top level — a name rather than 0.6's fence
// length, because that is what a closer now carries. Answers the index it stopped at.
function bmx_parse_blocks(lines: [BmxLine], from: Int, open: String, open_row: Int, mutable out: [Block]) -> Result<Int, String> {
    let mutable i: Int = from;
    while i < len(lines) {
        let line: BmxLine = lines[i];
        let text: String = bmx_strip_end(line.text);

        if bmx_is_blank(text) {
            i += 1;
        } else {
            // A tab in leading whitespace. Every dialect gives it a different width, so a format
            // promising one reading cannot accept it — even though the SPACES around it mean
            // nothing, a reader aligning by eye and a parser counting bytes would disagree about
            // what they are looking at.
            if len(text) > 0 && byte_at(text, 0) == 9 {
                return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E010", line.offset, "a tab in leading whitespace has no defined width"));
            }
            // **Indentation is insignificant, with exactly two exceptions** (§1). They are
            // exceptions because a list and a quote have no nesting AT ALL in this version, so an
            // indented one is not a second spelling of something legal — it is the illegal thing
            // with space in front of it.
            //
            // Until 0.5.1 this was a blanket refusal of every indented line, wearing the
            // nested-list message: `::: div` / `  hello` / `:::` was told *a list may not nest*
            // about a document with no list in it. That is the worst shape a diagnostic can have —
            // confident, specific, and naming something the reader did not write.
            // **An indented list or quote is NOT a nest, and 0.6 through 0.7 refused it as one.**
            // Found by round-tripping the suite through `tools/fmt.py`: indenting `:for:` / `- item`
            // produced *a list may not nest* about a document with one list in it, so the readable
            // form of the most motivating example was illegal in the release that existed to allow it.
            // A quote nests as `> > `, where indentation plays no part; a list nests when a marker is
            // DEEPER than the list already open, which is checked where the items are consumed.

            // ---- a 0.6 document, refused by name so nobody meets it as paragraph text --------
            if bmx_is_old_fence(text) {
                return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E036", line.offset,
                    "this is the 0.6 fence: 0.7 opens with `:name:` and closes with `:!name:`. Run `python3 tools/migrate-0.7.py` over the file"));
            }
            let closer: String = bmx_closer_name(text);
            if len(closer) > 0 {
                if len(open) == 0 {
                    return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E032", line.offset, "a closer with no open block"));
                }
                if closer != open {
                    // **The check that makes a named closer worth writing.** Without it `:!for:`
                    // against an open `button` would close the button anyway and the document would
                    // read as something it is not. A closer that can be wrong AND is believed is
                    // worse than the bare `:::` it replaced; refused, it can only be wrong.
                    // **Both positions, opened-at and closed-at.** A message with one position sends
                    // the reader back to counting, which is the work a named closer exists to delete.
                    return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E035", line.offset,
                        "`:!" + closer + ":` closes nothing here — the open block is `" + open
                        + "`, opened at " + to_string(open_row + 1) + ":"
                        + to_string(lines[open_row].indent + 1)
                        + ", so its closer is `:!" + open + ":`"));
                }
                return Result.Ok(i);
            }
            let marker: Int = bmx_marker_end(text);
            if marker > 0 {
                let name: String = substring(text, 1, marker - 1);
                if !html_is_name(name) {
                    // Without this a `:9lives:` is a paragraph beginning with a colon rather than a
                    // refusal — silence in the one place the format promises noise. The fixture
                    // `030-block-name-not-a-name` could not be migrated without it, which is how the
                    // hole was found.
                    return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E030", line.offset,
                        "a block name is a letter, then letters, digits, - and _"));
                }
                let mutable rest: String = substring(text, marker + 1, len(text) - (marker + 1));
                let target: String = ":!" + name + ":";
                let mutable one_liner: Bool = false;
                let mutable inline_body: String = "";
                let mutable bracket_at: Int = 0;
                // Where the inline body starts in the SOURCE. It is not the head's offset — passing that
                // put the body's nodes eleven bytes early, and `tests/agree.py` caught it because the
                // reference implementation computes the real position. A differential test cannot see a
                // rule both sides get wrong, but it sees this instantly.
                let mutable body_at: Int = 0;

                // ---- a DELIMITED head: `:name: -> [ … ] body` ----
                //
                // **What lets a body share the line with a head**, which nothing could before 0.9. A
                // head is opaque bytes, so `:span: class=text hello :!span:` sent `hello` into the head
                // and the text silently vanished. Andre's answer: the author says where the head stops.
                //
                // BMX still parses NOTHING inside the brackets — it learns one bracket pair as a
                // delimiter, and the commas and `name=value` stay the host's, exactly as before.
                let mutable arrow: Int = 0;
                while arrow < len(rest) && bmx_is_space(byte_at(rest, arrow)) {
                    arrow += 1;
                }
                let mutable delimited: Bool = false;
                if arrow + 2 < len(rest) && byte_at(rest, arrow) == 45 && byte_at(rest, arrow + 1) == 62 {
                    let mutable lbrack: Int = arrow + 2;
                    while lbrack < len(rest) && bmx_is_space(byte_at(rest, lbrack)) {
                        lbrack += 1;
                    }
                    if lbrack < len(rest) && byte_at(rest, lbrack) == 91 {
                        delimited = true;
                        let close: Int = bmx_head_end(rest, lbrack + 1);
                        if close < 0 {
                            return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E037", line.offset,
                                "a delimited head needs its `]`: `:" + name + ": -> [ … ]`"));
                        }
                        // The head ends at the FIRST `]`, which is the escape hatch rather than a
                        // defect: a host needing a `]` in a value writes the undelimited form, which
                        // takes the whole line and has no delimiter to collide with.
                        let after: String = substring(rest, close + 1, len(rest) - (close + 1));
                        bracket_at = lbrack + 1;
                        rest = substring(rest, lbrack + 1, close - (lbrack + 1));

                        let rear: String = bmx_strip_end(after);
                        if len(rear) >= len(target)
                           && substring(rear, len(rear) - len(target), len(target)) == target {
                            let raw_body: String = substring(rear, 0, len(rear) - len(target));
                            let mutable lead: Int = 0;
                            while lead < len(raw_body) && bmx_is_space(byte_at(raw_body, lead)) {
                                lead += 1;
                            }
                            body_at = line.offset + marker + 1 + close + 1 + lead;
                            inline_body = string_trim(raw_body);
                            one_liner = true;
                        } else {
                            if !bmx_is_blank(rear) {
                                // **Refused rather than merged.** Otherwise the body has two sources —
                                // this line and the lines below — and a reader cannot tell which won.
                                return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E038", line.offset,
                                    "a body after `]` needs the block to close on the same line — add `:!"
                                    + name + ":`, or put the body on the lines below"));
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }

                // The undelimited form, unchanged: a one-liner closes on its own line and everything
                // before the closer is head. Taken only at END of line and only when it names THIS
                // block, so a `:!x:` inside a head is left alone.
                if !delimited {
                    let trimmed: String = bmx_strip_end(rest);
                    if len(trimmed) >= len(target)
                       && substring(trimmed, len(trimmed) - len(target), len(target)) == target {
                        rest = substring(trimmed, 0, len(trimmed) - len(target));
                        one_liner = true;
                    }
                }
                let head: String = string_trim(rest);
                // At most one `#id`. Everything else in the head belongs to the host — but an id
                // is an ADDRESS, and a block with two of them has no answer to "where is it".
                let mutable ids: Int = 0;
                let mutable h: Int = 0;
                while h < len(head) {
                    // A comma separates head tokens as surely as a space does: `#one,#two` slipped
                    // past the whitespace-only version here AND in the reference implementation, so
                    // the differential test agreed on being wrong. A `#` inside a value —
                    // `href=/page#section` — must still not count, which is why this asks about the
                    // character before rather than counting every `#`.
                    let mutable before: Int = 32;
                    if h > 0 {
                        before = byte_at(head, h - 1);
                    }
                    let starts: Bool = bmx_is_space(before) || before == 44;
                    if starts && byte_at(head, h) == 35 && h + 1 < len(head) && html_is_name(substring(head, h + 1, 1)) {
                        ids += 1;
                    }
                    h += 1;
                }
                if ids > 1 {
                    return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E033", line.offset, "a block may carry at most one #id"));
                }
                let mutable pad: Int = 0;
                while pad < len(rest) && bmx_is_space(byte_at(rest, pad)) { pad += 1; }
                let head_at: Int = line.offset + marker + 1 + pad + bracket_at;
                let mutable kids: [Block] = [];
                if one_liner {
                    // **An UNDELIMITED one-liner's body is empty, and a delimited one's is not.** With
                    // no `-> [ … ]` the format cannot tell where `class=text` ended and `hello` began,
                    // so everything is head; with the delimiter the author has said, and what follows
                    // the `]` is inline content BMX parses and escapes — which is the whole difference
                    // between this and a host-side `child="…"`, where the body is a string the format
                    // never looks at and therefore never escapes.
                    if len(inline_body) > 0 {
                        match bmx_parse_inline(inline_body, body_at) {
                            Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                            Ok(part) => {
                                let para: Int = push(kids, Block.Paragraph(BmxWords {
                                    children: bmx_merge_text(part),
                                    offset: line.offset,
                                }));
                            }
                        }
                    }
                    let added: Int = push(out, Block.Fenced(BmxBlock {
                        name: name,
                        head: head,
                        one_line: true,
                        offset: line.offset,
                        head_offset: head_at,
                        children: kids,
                    }));
                    i += 1;
                } else {
                    match bmx_parse_blocks(lines, i + 1, name, i, kids) {
                        Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                        Ok(end) => {
                            if end >= len(lines) {
                                return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E031", line.offset,
                                    "unterminated block: no `:!" + name + ":`"));
                            }
                            let added: Int = push(out, Block.Fenced(BmxBlock {
                                name: name,
                                head: head,
                                one_line: false,
                                offset: line.offset,
                                head_offset: head_at,
                                children: kids,
                            }));
                            i = end + 1;
                        }
                    }
                }
            } else {
            if byte_at(text, 0) == 35 {
                let mutable level: Int = 0;
                while level < len(text) && byte_at(text, level) == 35 {
                    level += 1;
                }
                if level > 6 {
                    return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E011", line.offset, "a heading is one to six #"));
                }
                if level >= len(text) || byte_at(text, level) != 32 {
                    return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E011", line.offset, "a heading needs exactly one space after its #"));
                }
                let body: String = substring(text, level + 1, len(text) - level - 1);
                if len(body) == 0 {
                    return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E011", line.offset, "a heading may not be empty"));
                }
                match bmx_parse_inline(body, line.offset + level + 1) {
                    Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                    Ok(kids) => {
                        let added: Int = push(out, Block.Heading(BmxHeading { level: level, children: kids, offset: line.offset }));
                    }
                }
                i += 1;
            } else {
                if bmx_starts_with(text, "```") {
                    let info: String = substring(text, 3, len(text) - 3);
                    let mutable body: String = "";
                    let mutable j: Int = i + 1;
                    let mutable closed: Bool = false;
                    while j < len(lines) && !closed {
                        let inner: String = bmx_strip_end(lines[j].text);
                        if inner == "```" {
                            closed = true;
                        } else {
                            // The FENCE's indentation comes off; everything past it stays, so an
                            // indented fence holds exactly the code you would have written at
                            // column 0. Dedenting each line by its own indent would flatten it.
                            body = body + bmx_strip_fence_indent(lines[j].raw, line.indent) + "\n";
                        }
                        j += 1;
                    }
                    // Markdown closes an open fence at end of document, which is the single
                    // most common way a page becomes one giant code block with nobody told why.
                    if !closed {
                        return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E003", line.offset, "unterminated code fence"));
                    }
                    let added: Int = push(out, Block.Code(BmxCode { info: info, value: body, offset: line.offset }));
                    i = j;
                } else {
                    if bmx_starts_with(text, "> ") {
                        if bmx_starts_with(text, "> > ") {
                            return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-E012", line.offset, "a quote may not nest. A block nests — see the format's §4a.2"));
                        }
                        let mutable kids: [Bmx] = [];
                        let mutable j: Int = i;
                        while j < len(lines) && bmx_starts_with(bmx_strip_end(lines[j].text), "> ") {
                            let inner: String = bmx_strip_end(lines[j].text);
                            if j > i {
                                let broke: Int = push(kids, Bmx.Text("\n"));
                            }
                            match bmx_parse_inline(substring(inner, 2, len(inner) - 2), lines[j].offset + 2) {
                                Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                                Ok(part) => {
                                    let mutable k: Int = 0;
                                    while k < len(part) {
                                        let one: Int = push(kids, part[k]);
                                        k += 1;
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                            j += 1;
                        }
                        let added: Int = push(out, Block.Quote(BmxWords { children: bmx_merge_text(kids), offset: line.offset }));
                        i = j;
                    } else {
                        let ordered_at: Int = bmx_ordered_marker(text);
                        let unordered: Bool = bmx_starts_with(text, "- ");
                        if unordered || ordered_at > 0 {
                            let mutable items: [BmxItem] = [];
                            let mutable j: Int = i;
                            let mutable going: Bool = true;
                            while j < len(lines) && going {
                                let inner: String = bmx_strip_end(lines[j].text);
                                let here_ordered: Int = bmx_ordered_marker(inner);
                                let here_unordered: Bool = bmx_starts_with(inner, "- ");
                                let mutable skip: Int = 0 - 1;
                                if unordered && here_unordered { skip = 2; }
                                if !unordered && here_ordered > 0 { skip = here_ordered; }
                                if skip < 0 {
                                    going = false;
                                } else {
                                    // **The only place list nesting can be caught**: a continuation
                                    // line never reaches the top of the block loop, because this loop
                                    // consumes it. Deeper than the item that opened the list is a
                                    // nest; the same column or shallower is another item.
                                    if lines[j].indent > line.indent {
                                        return Result.Error(bmx_no_nesting(lines[j].offset, "list",
                                            "Put the `- ` in the same column as the item above it, or make it a block"));
                                    }
                                    match bmx_parse_inline(substring(inner, skip, len(inner) - skip), lines[j].offset + skip) {
                                        Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                                        Ok(kids) => {
                                            let added: Int = push(items, BmxItem { children: kids, offset: lines[j].offset });
                                        }
                                    }
                                    j += 1;
                                }
                            }
                            let added: Int = push(out, Block.List(BmxList { ordered: !unordered, items: items, offset: line.offset }));
                            i = j;
                        } else {
                            // A paragraph: consecutive non-blank lines that begin no other
                            // block, joined with a single newline.
                            let mutable kids: [Bmx] = [];
                            let mutable j: Int = i;
                            let mutable going: Bool = true;
                            while j < len(lines) && going {
                                let inner: String = bmx_strip_end(lines[j].text);
                                let starts_block: Bool = bmx_is_blank(inner)
                                    || bmx_marker_end(inner) > 0 || len(bmx_closer_name(inner)) > 0
                                    || bmx_is_old_fence(inner)
                                    || bmx_starts_with(inner, "# ") || bmx_starts_with(inner, "#")
                                    || bmx_starts_with(inner, "```") || bmx_starts_with(inner, "> ")
                                    || bmx_starts_with(inner, "- ") || bmx_ordered_marker(inner) > 0;
                                if j > i && starts_block {
                                    going = false;
                                } else {
                                    if j > i {
                                        let broke: Int = push(kids, Bmx.Text("\n"));
                                    }
                                    match bmx_parse_inline(inner, lines[j].offset) {
                                        Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                                        Ok(part) => {
                                            let mutable k: Int = 0;
                                            while k < len(part) {
                                                let one: Int = push(kids, part[k]);
                                                k += 1;
                                            }
                                        }
                                    }
                                    j += 1;
                                }
                            }
                            let added: Int = push(out, Block.Paragraph(BmxWords { children: bmx_merge_text(kids), offset: line.offset }));
                            i = j;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            }
        }
    }
    return Result.Ok(i);
}

// ---- the AST as JSON -----------------------------------------------------------
//
// This is what the format's conformance suite compares. It is not for humans and it is not the
// rendering path — it exists so that two implementations can be shown to agree about STRUCTURE
// rather than about the pages someone thought to look at.

function bmx_inline_json(nodes: [Bmx]) -> Json {
    let mutable out: [Json] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(nodes) {
        match nodes[i] {
            Text(value) => {
                let added: Int = push(out, json_object([
                    json_field("type", json_text("text")),
                    json_field("value", json_text(value)),
                ]));
            }
            Emphasis(wrap) => {
                let added: Int = push(out, json_object([
                    json_field("type", json_text("emphasis")),
                    json_field("children", bmx_inline_json(wrap.children)),
                    json_field("offset", json_int(wrap.offset)),
                ]));
            }
            Strong(wrap) => {
                let added: Int = push(out, json_object([
                    json_field("type", json_text("strong")),
                    json_field("children", bmx_inline_json(wrap.children)),
                    json_field("offset", json_int(wrap.offset)),
                ]));
            }
            CodeSpan(value) => {
                let added: Int = push(out, json_object([
                    json_field("type", json_text("code_span")),
                    json_field("value", json_text(value)),
                ]));
            }
            Link(link) => {
                let added: Int = push(out, json_object([
                    json_field("type", json_text("link")),
                    json_field("target", json_text(link.target)),
                    json_field("children", bmx_inline_json(link.children)),
                    json_field("offset", json_int(link.offset)),
                ]));
            }
            Slot(slot) => {
                let added: Int = push(out, json_object([
                    json_field("type", json_text("slot")),
                    json_field("expression", json_text(slot.expression)),
                    json_field("offset", json_int(slot.offset)),
                ]));
            }
            InlineBlock(call) => {
                let added: Int = push(out, json_object([
                    json_field("type", json_text("inline_block")),
                    json_field("name", json_text(call.name)),
                    json_field("head", json_text(call.head)),
                    json_field("offset", json_int(call.offset)),
                    json_field("head_offset", json_int(call.head_offset)),
                ]));
            }
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return json_list(out);
}

// The children array on its own, because a block carries one and the document carries one.
function bmx_json_children(blocks: [Block]) -> Json {
    let mutable out: [Json] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(blocks) {
        match blocks[i] {
            Heading(heading) => {
                let added: Int = push(out, json_object([
                    json_field("type", json_text("heading")),
                    json_field("level", json_int(heading.level)),
                    json_field("children", bmx_inline_json(heading.children)),
                    json_field("offset", json_int(heading.offset)),
                ]));
            }
            Paragraph(words) => {
                let added: Int = push(out, json_object([
                    json_field("type", json_text("paragraph")),
                    json_field("children", bmx_inline_json(words.children)),
                    json_field("offset", json_int(words.offset)),
                ]));
            }
            Quote(words) => {
                let added: Int = push(out, json_object([
                    json_field("type", json_text("quote")),
                    json_field("children", bmx_inline_json(words.children)),
                    json_field("offset", json_int(words.offset)),
                ]));
            }
            List(list) => {
                let mutable items: [Json] = [];
                let mutable k: Int = 0;
                while k < len(list.items) {
                    let one: Int = push(items, json_object([
                        json_field("type", json_text("item")),
                        json_field("children", bmx_inline_json(list.items[k].children)),
                        json_field("offset", json_int(list.items[k].offset)),
                    ]));
                    k += 1;
                }
                let added: Int = push(out, json_object([
                    json_field("type", json_text("list")),
                    json_field("ordered", json_truth(list.ordered)),
                    json_field("items", json_list(items)),
                    json_field("offset", json_int(list.offset)),
                ]));
            }
            Code(code) => {
                let added: Int = push(out, json_object([
                    json_field("type", json_text("code")),
                    json_field("info", json_text(code.info)),
                    json_field("value", json_text(code.value)),
                    json_field("offset", json_int(code.offset)),
                ]));
            }
            Fenced(fenced) => {
                let added: Int = push(out, json_object([
                    json_field("type", json_text("block")),
                    json_field("name", json_text(fenced.name)),
                    json_field("head", json_text(fenced.head)),
                    json_field("one_line", json_truth(fenced.one_line)),
                    json_field("offset", json_int(fenced.offset)),
                    json_field("head_offset", json_int(fenced.head_offset)),
                    json_field("children", bmx_json_children(fenced.children)),
                ]));
            }
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return json_list(out);
}

function bmx_json(blocks: [Block]) -> Json {
    return json_object([
        json_field("type", json_text("document")),
        json_field("children", bmx_json_children(blocks)),
    ]);
}

// ---- rendering, level 1 --------------------------------------------------------
//
// Parse, then substitute. This is the conformance level any language can reach, and it is
// honest about what it is: slot values are looked up by their expression TEXT, so nothing here
// checks a type. Level 2 — every slot checked against a declared interface before the document
// renders — is not a library and cannot be: in Burxt it is a generator that turns a document
// into a `pure function ... -> Html` whose slots are ordinary expressions, and then the
// compiler does the checking with types, contracts and the rounding rule already in hand.
// That generator is the next slice and it is NOT in this file.
//
// What this level still refuses, because refusing is cheap and a blank page is not:
//
//   * a slot whose expression has no binding — a missing value is an error, never an empty
//     string. Every template language in wide use renders the empty string here, and that is
//     how a page ships with a silently absent total.
//   * a link target whose scheme is not allowed. `javascript:` is a working attack that no
//     amount of character escaping addresses, because the danger is the scheme and not the
//     bytes. `ESCAPING.md` makes this the host's duty and the conformance suite asserts it.

class Binding { name: String, value: String }

pure function bmx_bind(name: String, value: String) -> Binding {
    return Binding { name: name, value: value };
}

pure function bmx_lookup(bindings: [Binding], name: String) -> Option<String> {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(bindings) {
        if bindings[i].name == name {
            return Option.Some(bindings[i].value);
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return Option.None;
}

// A target with no scheme is relative and allowed. A target with a scheme is allowed only from
// a named set. The check is "is there a `:` before the first `/`", which is what distinguishes
// `mailto:a@b` and `javascript:x` from `/page` and `a/b:c`.
pure function bmx_target_allowed(target: String) -> Bool {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    let n: Int = len(target);
    while i < n {
        let b: Int = byte_at(target, i);
        if b == 47 { return true; }
        if b == 58 {
            // **Folded, because a URI scheme is case-insensitive** (RFC 3986 §3.1): refusing
            // `HTTPS://example.com` refuses a correct URL, which is a false refusal of correct input —
            // the one class a conformance suite cannot hold, since nobody writes a case asserting that
            // something they believe illegal actually works.
            //
            // Folding cannot open a hole because this is an ALLOW-list: it admits only case-variants of
            // the three schemes below, and `JAVASCRIPT:` folds to `javascript`, still absent. ASCII-only.
            let scheme: String = string_to_lower_ascii(substring(target, 0, i));
            if scheme == "http" { return true; }
            if scheme == "https" { return true; }
            if scheme == "mailto" { return true; }
            return false;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return true;
}

function bmx_inline_html(nodes: [Bmx], bindings: [Binding]) -> Result<[Html], String> {
    let mutable out: [Html] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(nodes) {
        match nodes[i] {
            Text(value) => {
                let added: Int = push(out, html_text(value));
            }
            Emphasis(wrap) => {
                match bmx_inline_html(wrap.children, bindings) {
                    Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                    Ok(kids) => { let added: Int = push(out, html_element("em", [], kids)); }
                }
            }
            Strong(wrap) => {
                match bmx_inline_html(wrap.children, bindings) {
                    Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                    Ok(kids) => { let added: Int = push(out, html_element("strong", [], kids)); }
                }
            }
            CodeSpan(value) => {
                let added: Int = push(out, html_element("code", [], [html_text(value)]));
            }
            Link(link) => {
                if !bmx_target_allowed(link.target) {
                    return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-R001", link.offset,
                        "refused a link target whose scheme is not http, https or mailto: " + link.target));
                }
                match bmx_inline_html(link.children, bindings) {
                    Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                    Ok(kids) => {
                        let added: Int = push(out, html_element("a", [html_attr("href", link.target)], kids));
                    }
                }
            }
            Slot(slot) => {
                match bmx_lookup(bindings, slot.expression) {
                    None => {
                        return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-R002", slot.offset,
                            "no binding for slot `" + slot.expression + "`"));
                    }
                    Some(value) => {
                        // `html_text`, never `html_raw`: a slot's value is escaped on render,
                        // always, and BMX has no syntax that opts out of that.
                        let added: Int = push(out, html_text(value));
                    }
                }
            }
            // SPEC 4a.5: a host must refuse a block name it did not declare, and never render it
            // or skip it silently. This renderer declares none — components are a level-2 idea,
            // because their whole value is the compiler checking the call.
            InlineBlock(call) => {
                return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-R003", call.offset,
                    "this renderer declares no blocks, and `" + call.name
                    + "` is one. Compile the document instead."));
            }
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return Result.Ok(out);
}

pure function bmx_heading_tag(level: Int) -> String {
    return "h" + to_string(level);
}

function bmx_html(blocks: [Block], bindings: [Binding]) -> Result<Html, String> {
    let mutable out: [Html] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(blocks) {
        match blocks[i] {
            Heading(heading) => {
                match bmx_inline_html(heading.children, bindings) {
                    Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                    Ok(kids) => {
                        let added: Int = push(out, html_element(bmx_heading_tag(heading.level), [], kids));
                    }
                }
            }
            Paragraph(words) => {
                match bmx_inline_html(words.children, bindings) {
                    Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                    Ok(kids) => { let added: Int = push(out, html_element("p", [], kids)); }
                }
            }
            Quote(words) => {
                match bmx_inline_html(words.children, bindings) {
                    Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                    Ok(kids) => { let added: Int = push(out, html_element("blockquote", [], kids)); }
                }
            }
            List(list) => {
                let mutable items: [Html] = [];
                let mutable k: Int = 0;
                while k < len(list.items) {
                    match bmx_inline_html(list.items[k].children, bindings) {
                        Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                        Ok(kids) => { let one: Int = push(items, html_element("li", [], kids)); }
                    }
                    k += 1;
                }
                let mutable tag: String = "ul";
                if list.ordered { tag = "ol"; }
                let added: Int = push(out, html_element(tag, [], items));
            }
            Fenced(fenced) => {
                return Result.Error(bmx_error("BMX-R003", fenced.offset,
                    "this renderer declares no blocks, and `" + fenced.name
                    + "` is one. Compile the document instead."));
            }
            Code(code) => {
                // The info string becomes a class the way every highlighter expects, and it is
                // checked as a NAME first — an info string is author text, and an unchecked one
                // would be the markup-injection hole `html_is_name` exists to close.
                let mutable attrs: [Attr] = [];
                if len(code.info) > 0 && html_is_name(code.info) {
                    let one: Int = push(attrs, html_attr("class", "language-" + code.info));
                }
                let inner: Html = html_element("code", attrs, [html_text(code.value)]);
                let added: Int = push(out, html_element("pre", [], [inner]));
            }
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return Result.Ok(html_element("article", [html_attr("class", "bmx")], out));
}

// Source in, page out. The whole path, for the caller who does not need the tree.
function bmx_to_html(source: String, bindings: [Binding]) -> Result<String, String> {
    match bmx_parse(source) {
        Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
        Ok(blocks) => {
            match bmx_html(blocks, bindings) {
                Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                Ok(page) => { return Result.Ok(html_render(page)); }
            }
        }
    }
}

// ---- level 2: emitting Burxt ---------------------------------------------------
//
// **This is the half no other host can implement, and it is a code generator rather than a
// library on purpose.** A document becomes a `pure function ... -> Html` whose slots are
// ordinary Burxt expressions, and then the COMPILER checks them. Nothing below knows what a
// type is; it does not have to. That is `BOUNDARY.md` paying off.
//
// What the compiler then catches, none of which is implemented here:
//
//   * a slot naming a field that does not exist — a type error, at the expression;
//   * a slot holding something that is not a `String` — `html_text` takes a `String`, and
//     `to_string` on a String is refused, so **the conversion is written in the document**
//     (`\{\{ to_string(line.each) \}\}`) where a reviewer sees it. That is not a wart: it is the
//     same rule the language applies everywhere, arriving in the template;
//   * money that would silently re-round — `Decimal<2, RoundHalfEven>` keeps its scale and tie
//     rule into the tag, and a multiplication that needs a contract is refused in the view;
//   * a weakened promise between versions — `burxt review` diffs the generated signature.
//
// The view's SIGNATURE comes from outside the document, not from front matter. `SPEC.md` §7
// leaves front matter deliberately unbuilt, and a generator that invented one would be adding
// to the format from the host side — exactly the leak `BOUNDARY.md` exists to prevent.

// A Burxt string literal, escaped. `\{` and `\}` are the two a reader will not expect: a bare
// brace opens or closes an interpolation, so a document's own braces have to survive the trip
// into a literal.
pure function bmx_burxt_string(text: String) -> String {
    let mutable out: String = "\"";
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(text) {
        let b: Int = byte_at(text, i);
        if b == 92 { out = out + "\\\\"; }
        if b == 34 { out = out + "\\\""; }
        if b == 10 { out = out + "\\n"; }
        if b == 123 { out = out + "\\\{"; }
        if b == 125 { out = out + "\\\}"; }
        let plain: Bool = b != 92 && b != 34 && b != 10 && b != 123 && b != 125;
        if plain { out = out + byte_as_string(b); }
        i += 1;
    }
    return out + "\"";
}

public function bmx_emit_inline(nodes: [Bmx]) -> Result<String, String> {
    let mutable out: String = "[";
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(nodes) {
        if i > 0 { out = out + ", "; }
        match nodes[i] {
            Text(value) => { out = out + "html_text(" + bmx_burxt_string(value) + ")"; }
            Emphasis(wrap) => {
                match bmx_emit_inline(wrap.children) {
                    Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                    Ok(kids) => { out = out + "html_element(\"em\", [], " + kids + ")"; }
                }
            }
            Strong(wrap) => {
                match bmx_emit_inline(wrap.children) {
                    Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                    Ok(kids) => { out = out + "html_element(\"strong\", [], " + kids + ")"; }
                }
            }
            CodeSpan(value) => {
                out = out + "html_element(\"code\", [], [html_text(" + bmx_burxt_string(value) + ")])";
            }
            Link(link) => {
                // Refused at BUILD time, not at render time. A document's link targets are
                // static text, so a `javascript:` target is a thing the generator can simply
                // decline to emit — which is strictly better than catching it on a page view.
                if !bmx_target_allowed(link.target) {
                    return Result.Error("BMX-G001: refused a link target whose scheme is not http, https or mailto: " + link.target);
                }
                match bmx_emit_inline(link.children) {
                    Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                    Ok(kids) => {
                        out = out + "html_element(\"a\", [html_attr(\"href\", "
                            + bmx_burxt_string(link.target) + ")], " + kids + ")";
                    }
                }
            }
            // The whole point. The expression goes through VERBATIM and the compiler judges it.
            Slot(slot) => { out = out + "html_text(" + slot.expression + ")"; }
            // An inline block is a call to something the host declared. Its head is host CODE,
            // like every other head — so `::icon["trash"]::`, not `::icon[trash]::`. One rule for
            // what a head is beats a second rule for one construct.
            InlineBlock(call) => { out = out + call.name + "(" + call.head + ")"; }
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return Result.Ok(out + "]");
}

// The expression for one block that is NOT a fence. Fences are statements and are handled by
// `bmx_emit_stmts`, which is the only caller.
public function bmx_emit_one(block: Block) -> Result<String, String> {
    let mutable out: String = "";
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < 1 {
        match block {
            Heading(heading) => {
                match bmx_emit_inline(heading.children) {
                    Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                    Ok(kids) => {
                        out = out + "html_element(\"" + bmx_heading_tag(heading.level) + "\", [], " + kids + ")";
                    }
                }
            }
            Paragraph(words) => {
                match bmx_emit_inline(words.children) {
                    Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                    Ok(kids) => { out = out + "html_element(\"p\", [], " + kids + ")"; }
                }
            }
            Quote(words) => {
                match bmx_emit_inline(words.children) {
                    Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                    Ok(kids) => { out = out + "html_element(\"blockquote\", [], " + kids + ")"; }
                }
            }
            List(list) => {
                let mutable items: String = "[";
                let mutable k: Int = 0;
                while k < len(list.items) {
                    if k > 0 { items = items + ", "; }
                    match bmx_emit_inline(list.items[k].children) {
                        Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                        Ok(kids) => { items = items + "html_element(\"li\", [], " + kids + ")"; }
                    }
                    k += 1;
                }
                let mutable tag: String = "ul";
                if list.ordered { tag = "ol"; }
                out = out + "html_element(\"" + tag + "\", [], " + items + "])";
            }
            Code(code) => {
                let mutable attrs: String = "[]";
                if len(code.info) > 0 && html_is_name(code.info) {
                    attrs = "[html_attr(\"class\", \"language-" + code.info + "\")]";
                }
                out = out + "html_element(\"pre\", [], [html_element(\"code\", " + attrs
                    + ", [html_text(" + bmx_burxt_string(code.value) + ")])])";
            }
            // A fence is a STATEMENT — `for` and `if` bind names — so `bmx_emit_stmts` handles
            // it and never calls this with one. Burxt makes every variant explicit, which turns
            // that fact into something a reader can see rather than something two functions
            // apart have to agree about.
            Fenced(fenced) => {
                return Result.Error("BMX-G003: internal — a fence reached the expression emitter");
            }
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return Result.Ok(out);
}

// Statements that push each block into `target`, because a fence is a statement: `for` and `if`
// bind names, and a binding cannot live inside an expression.
//
// **This is where the whole design pays off.** `::: for line in order.lines` becomes a real Burxt
// `for`, so inside the body `line` is a real `Line` — a slot saying `line.sk` is a type error
// naming the field, and money that would round without a contract is refused. No other template
// language checks the body of a loop, because no other one hands that body to a compiler which
// already knows the types.
//
// `tag` makes generated names unique without threading a counter: children of block `i` under
// `tag` are built in `k_<tag>_<i>`.
public function bmx_emit_stmts(blocks: [Block], target: String, tag: String, indent: String) -> Result<String, String> {
    let mutable out: String = "";
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(blocks) {
        let label: String = "p_" + tag + "_" + to_string(i);
        match blocks[i] {
            Fenced(fenced) => {
                let inner: String = "k_" + tag + "_" + to_string(i);
                if fenced.name == "for" || fenced.name == "if" {
                    // The head is host CODE and goes through verbatim — `line in order.lines`,
                    // `order.paid`. The body pushes into the SAME list, because neither construct
                    // produces an element of its own: they repeat or omit what is already there.
                    match bmx_emit_stmts(fenced.children, target, tag + "_" + to_string(i), indent + "    ") {
                        Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                        Ok(body) => {
                            out = out + indent + fenced.name + " " + fenced.head + " \{\n" + body + indent + "\}\n";
                        }
                    }
                } else {
                    // A component. Children first, then handed to it with its head as TEXT — the
                    // head is the format's to capture and the component's to read, which is the
                    // same split as everywhere else.
                    match bmx_emit_stmts(fenced.children, inner, tag + "_" + to_string(i), indent) {
                        Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                        Ok(body) => {
                            out = out + indent + "let mutable " + inner + ": [Html] = [];\n" + body;
                            out = out + indent + "let " + label + ": Int = push(" + target + ", "
                                + fenced.name + "(" + bmx_burxt_string(fenced.head) + ", " + inner + "));\n";
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            Heading(heading) => {
                match bmx_emit_one(blocks[i]) {
                    Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                    Ok(expr) => { out = out + indent + "let " + label + ": Int = push(" + target + ", " + expr + ");\n"; }
                }
            }
            Paragraph(words) => {
                match bmx_emit_one(blocks[i]) {
                    Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                    Ok(expr) => { out = out + indent + "let " + label + ": Int = push(" + target + ", " + expr + ");\n"; }
                }
            }
            Quote(words) => {
                match bmx_emit_one(blocks[i]) {
                    Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                    Ok(expr) => { out = out + indent + "let " + label + ": Int = push(" + target + ", " + expr + ");\n"; }
                }
            }
            List(list) => {
                match bmx_emit_one(blocks[i]) {
                    Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                    Ok(expr) => { out = out + indent + "let " + label + ": Int = push(" + target + ", " + expr + ");\n"; }
                }
            }
            Code(code) => {
                match bmx_emit_one(blocks[i]) {
                    Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
                    Ok(expr) => { out = out + indent + "let " + label + ": Int = push(" + target + ", " + expr + ");\n"; }
                }
            }
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return Result.Ok(out);
}

// A `props` block declares the view's own signature, so a component can be invoked by another
// document without an out-of-band agreement about its parameters:
//
//     ::: props title: String, featured: Bool
//     :::
//
// It is not a new construct — `props` is a block name like any other, and its head is captured
// opaquely exactly like `for`'s. **That is why §7's front-matter row closed as NOT NEEDED rather
// than as built**: the format already had the general shape, and the answer was to use it on
// itself.
//
// Answers the head of the first `props` block, or "" if the document declares none.
function bmx_props(blocks: [Block]) -> String {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(blocks) {
        match blocks[i] {
            Fenced(fenced) => {
                if fenced.name == "props" { return fenced.head; }
            }
            Heading(h) => { }
            Paragraph(w) => { }
            Quote(w) => { }
            List(l) => { }
            Code(c) => { }
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return "";
}

// The document without its `props` block, because a declaration is not content and must not
// become a call to a component nobody wrote.
function bmx_without_props(blocks: [Block]) -> [Block] {
    let mutable out: [Block] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(blocks) {
        let mutable keep: Bool = true;
        match blocks[i] {
            Fenced(fenced) => {
                if fenced.name == "props" { keep = false; }
            }
            Heading(h) => { }
            Paragraph(w) => { }
            Quote(w) => { }
            List(l) => { }
            Code(c) => { }
        }
        if keep {
            let added: Int = push(out, blocks[i]);
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// A document and a signature in, a Burxt source file out.
//
// `parameters` is written verbatim into the signature (`"order: Order"`) and `requires` is one
// clause per entry. Both come from the caller because the FORMAT does not carry them — see the
// note above about front matter.
function bmx_emit_burxt(blocks: [Block], source_name: String, name: String, parameters: String, clauses: [String]) -> Result<String, String> {
    // The document's own `props` block wins over the caller's argument, because a component that
    // declares its interface is the one thing that lets another document invoke it.
    let declared: String = bmx_props(blocks);
    let mutable signature: String = parameters;
    if len(declared) > 0 { signature = declared; }
    match bmx_emit_stmts(bmx_without_props(blocks), "kids", "0", "    ") {
        Error(e) => { return Result.Error(e); }
        Ok(body) => {
            let mutable out: String = "// GENERATED from " + source_name + " by `bmx generate`. Do not edit.\n";
            out = out + "//\n";
            out = out + "// Every slot in the document became an ordinary Burxt expression below, so the\n";
            out = out + "// compiler checks it: a missing field is a type error, and a rounding contract\n";
            out = out + "// survives into the tag. That is what the document could not do by itself.\n";
            // **`std/`, not `lib/`.** A generated view is written wherever its author wants it,
            // and `use "lib/html.bx"` resolves relative to THAT file — so it only worked when
            // somebody arranged a `lib/` beside the output, which the old test did with a symlink.
            // `std/` names the standard library from anywhere, which is the whole reason it exists,
            // and it is the only form that works for a consumer who reached this generator through
            // a package rather than through Burxt's own tree.
            out = out + "use \"std/html.bx\";\n\n";
            // **Brace placement follows the language's own convention, measured rather than guessed.**
            // Across Burxt 1.3.0's `lib/`: 338 functions put `{` on the signature line, 79 put it on
            // its own line with contract clauses between, and 4 do neither. So the rule is *the brace
            // hangs only when a clause does* — and this generator was in the 4. Generated code that
            // reads differently from hand-written code puts two styles in front of one reviewer,
            // which is a cost paid by the person the format exists to serve.
            out = out + "pure function " + name + "(" + signature + ") -> Html";
            if len(clauses) == 0 {
                out = out + " \{\n";
            } else {
                out = out + "\n";
                let mutable i: Int = 0;
                while i < len(clauses) {
                    out = out + "    requires " + clauses[i] + "\n";
                    i += 1;
                }
                out = out + "\{\n";
            }
            out = out + "    let mutable kids: [Html] = [];\n";
            out = out + body;
            out = out + "    return html_element(\"article\", [html_attr(\"class\", \"bmx\")], kids);\n";
            out = out + "\}\n";
            return Result.Ok(out);
        }
    }
}
